{
  "dom_id": "step-531825",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor",
  "problem": "A computer has $10$ files infected with a computer virus. If the number of infected files quadruples every hour, find a function that gives the number of infected files after $t$ hours. How many infected files will there be after $6$ hours?",
  "worked_solution": "The number of infected files, $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 10$ is the initial number of infected files\n\n- $b = 4$ is the growth factor (the number of infected files quadruples)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours)\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny = ab^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 10 \\cdot 4^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the number of files infected after $6$ hours, we  substitute $t=6$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(6) = 10 \\cdot 4^{6} = 40\\,960\n$$\n\nTherefore, the computer will have $40\\,960$ infected files after $6$ hours.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
